{
  "beruf": "bau-handwerk",
  "title": "Construction and trades",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "spanish-for-work run, 2026-11-07: written by hand for Spain, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon or in another deck are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Building sites and home repairs are a common way into work in Spain. This deck has the site and its tools, what you do there, the orders you will hear, safety equipment, and how to talk to clients and the team. On site nearly everyone says tú, and instructions are short.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work on a building site or do repairs in Spain? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Peón de albañil",
    "Albañil",
    "Pintor/a",
    "Fontanero/a",
    "Electricista",
    "Encofrador/a",
    "Yesista"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Orders on site are short: «¡Sube!», «¡Baja!», «¡Para!», «¡Más a la derecha!». If you don't understand, ask before you move: «¿Cómo? ¿Me lo repites?»",
    "Don't work without your EPI (helmet, boots, harness). You can ask: «¿Dónde está el casco?» Asking is normal, not a complaint.",
    "Construction work in Spain usually asks for a basic safety course (often called the curso de 20 horas or the TPC card). Ask the company what it needs. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
